#: ../../../source/user_guide/fundamentals/public_api.rst:3
msgid "Public API"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/user_guide/fundamentals/public_api.rst:8
msgid ""
"PyBaMM is a Python package for mathematical modelling and simulation of "
"battery systems. The main classes and functions that are intended to be used "
"by the user are described in this document. For a more detailed description "
"of the classes and methods, see the :doc:`API reference </source/api/index>`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/user_guide/fundamentals/public_api.rst:12
msgid "Available PyBaMM models"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/user_guide/fundamentals/public_api.rst:14
msgid ""
"PyBaMM includes a number of pre-implemented models, which can be used as "
"they are or modified to suit your needs. The main models are:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/user_guide/fundamentals/public_api.rst:16
msgid ":class:`lithium_ion.SPM`: Single Particle Model"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/user_guide/fundamentals/public_api.rst:17
msgid ":class:`lithium_ion.SPMe`: Single Particle Model with Electrolyte"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/user_guide/fundamentals/public_api.rst:18
msgid ":class:`lithium_ion.DFN`: Doyle-Fuller-Newman"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/user_guide/fundamentals/public_api.rst:20
msgid ""
"The behaviour of the models can be modified by passing in an :class:"
"`BatteryModelOptions` object when creating the model."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/user_guide/fundamentals/public_api.rst:23
msgid "Simulations"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/user_guide/fundamentals/public_api.rst:25
msgid ""
":class:`Simulation` is a class that automates the process of setting up a "
"model and solving it, and acts as the highest-level API to PyBaMM. Pass at "
"least a :class:`BaseModel` object, and optionally the experiment, solver, "
"parameter values, and geometry objects described below to the :class:"
"`Simulation` object. Any of these optional arguments not provided will be "
"supplied by the defaults specified in the model."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/user_guide/fundamentals/public_api.rst:30
msgid "Parameters"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/user_guide/fundamentals/public_api.rst:32
msgid ""
"PyBaMM models are parameterised by a set of parameters, which are stored in "
"a :class:`ParameterValues` object. This object acts like a Python dictionary "
"with a few extra PyBaMM specific features and methods. Parameters in a model "
"are represented as either :class:`Parameter` objects or :class:"
"`FunctionParameter` objects, and the values in the :class:`ParameterValues` "
"object replace these objects in the model before it is solved. The values in "
"the :class:`ParameterValues` object can be scalars, Python functions or "
"expressions of type :class:`Symbol`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/user_guide/fundamentals/public_api.rst:37
msgid "Experiments"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/user_guide/fundamentals/public_api.rst:39
msgid ""
"An :class:`Experiment` object represents an experimental protocol that can "
"be used to simulate the behaviour of a battery. The particular protocol can "
"be provided as a Python string, or as a sequences of :class:`step.BaseStep` "
"objects."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/user_guide/fundamentals/public_api.rst:43
msgid "Solvers"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/user_guide/fundamentals/public_api.rst:45
msgid ""
"The two main solvers in PyBaMM are the :class:`CasadiSolver` and the :class:"
"`IDAKLUSolver`. Both are wrappers around the Sundials suite of solvers, but "
"the :class:`CasadiSolver` uses the CasADi library whereas the :class:"
"`IDAKLUSolver` is PyBaMM specific. Both solvers have many options that can "
"be set to control the solver behaviour, see the documentation for each "
"solver for more details."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/user_guide/fundamentals/public_api.rst:48
msgid ""
"When a model is solved, the solution is returned as a :class:`Solution` "
"object."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/user_guide/fundamentals/public_api.rst:51
msgid "Plotting"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/user_guide/fundamentals/public_api.rst:53
msgid ""
"A solution object can be plotted using the :meth:`Solution.plot` or :meth:"
"`Simulation.plot` methods, which returns a :class:`QuickPlot` object. Note "
"that the arguments to the plotting methods of both classes are the same as :"
"class:`QuickPlot`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/user_guide/fundamentals/public_api.rst:56
msgid ""
"Other plotting functions are the :func:`plot_voltage_components` and :func:"
"`plot_summary_variables` functions, which correspond to the similarly named "
"methods of the :class:`Solution` and :class:`Simulation` classes."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/user_guide/fundamentals/public_api.rst:59
msgid "Writing PyBaMM models"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/user_guide/fundamentals/public_api.rst:61
msgid ""
"Each PyBaMM model, and the custom models written by users, are written as a "
"set of expressions that describe the model. Each of the expressions is a "
"subclass of the :class:`Symbol` class, which represents a mathematical "
"expression."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/user_guide/fundamentals/public_api.rst:63
msgid ""
"If you wish to create a custom model, you can use the :class:`BaseModel` "
"class as a starting point."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/user_guide/fundamentals/public_api.rst:67
msgid "Discretisation"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/user_guide/fundamentals/public_api.rst:69
msgid ""
"Each PyBaMM model contains continuous operators that must be discretised "
"before they can be solved. This is done using a :class:`Discretisation` "
"object, which takes a :class:`Mesh` object and a dictionary of :class:"
"`SpatialMethod` objects."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/user_guide/fundamentals/public_api.rst:72
msgid "Logging"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/user_guide/fundamentals/public_api.rst:74
msgid ""
"PyBaMM uses the Python logging module to log messages at different levels of "
"severity. Use the :func:`pybamm.set_logging_level` function to set the "
"logging level for PyBaMM."
msgstr ""
